# Introduction


# Data Construction and Exploration

## Data Collection

We obtained data from the 2005–2006 cycle of the National Health and 
Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) using the `nhanesA` package. 
We selected six modules to examine the relationship between self-rated 
general health and demographic characteristics, physical activity, 
body measurements, and medical conditions.

```{r}
library(nhanesA)
library(dplyr)
library(purrr)
library(readr)
```

```{r}
demo_raw <- nhanes("DEMO_D")  # Demographics
huq_raw <- nhanes("HUQ_D")    # Health status/access to care
bmx_raw <- nhanes("BMX_D")    # Body measurements
paq_raw <- nhanes("PAQ_D")    # Physical activity
diq_raw <- nhanes("DIQ_D")    # Diabetes
mcq_raw <- nhanes("MCQ_D")    # Medical conditions
```

## Variable Selection

We selected potential predictors from each module based on their 
relevance to self-rated general health. The respondent identification 
number (SEQN) was retained in each dataset to allow the modules to 
be merged.

```{r demographics}
# Select demographic and socioeconomic predictors
demo <- demo_raw %>%
  select(
    RIAGENDR,  # Gender
    RIDAGEYR,  # Age in years
    RIDRETH1,  # Race/Hispanic origin
    DMDEDUC2,  # Education level
    DMDMARTL,  # Marital status
    INDFMPIR,  # Family income-to-poverty ratio
    INDHHINC,  # Total household income
    SEQN
  )
```

```{r body-measurements}
# Select waist circumference as a measure of body composition
bmx <- bmx_raw %>%
  select(
    BMXWAIST,  # Waist circumference (cm)
    SEQN
  )
```

```{r health-status}
# Select the response variable
huq <- huq_raw %>%
  select(
    HUQ010,  # Self-rated general health
    SEQN
  )
```

```{r physical-activity}
# Select physical activity predictors
paq <- paq_raw %>%
  select(
    PAD320,  # Moderate physical activity
    PAD440,  # Muscle-strengthening activity
    PAQ180,  # Usual daily activity level
    SEQN
  )
```

```{r diabetes}
# Select diabetes diagnosis status
diq <- diq_raw %>%
  select(
    DIQ010,  # Ever diagnosed with diabetes
    SEQN
  )
```

```{r medical-conditions}
# Select medical history and chronic condition predictors
mcq <- mcq_raw %>%
  select(
    MCQ035,   # Current asthma
    MCQ092,   # History of blood transfusion
    MCQ160A,  # Arthritis
    MCQ160B,  # Congestive heart failure
    MCQ160C,  # Coronary heart disease
    MCQ160D,  # Angina
    MCQ160E,  # Heart attack
    MCQ160F,  # Stroke
    MCQ160G,  # Emphysema
    MCQ160K,  # Chronic bronchitis
    MCQ160L,  # Liver condition
    MCQ160M,  # Thyroid problems
    MCQ220,   # Cancer
    SEQN
  )
```

## Merging the Datasets

The six datasets were merged using SEQN, a unique identification 
number assigned to each NHANES participant. We used full joins to 
retain participants even if they did not have responses in every 
selected module.

```{r}
# Combine the selected datasets into a list
df_list <- list(
  demo = demo, bmx = bmx, huq = huq,
  paq = paq, diq = diq, mcq = mcq
)

# Examine module sample sizes before merging
sapply(df_list, nrow)

# Verify that participant IDs are unique within each module
stopifnot(all(sapply(df_list, function(x) {
  !anyDuplicated(x$SEQN)
})))

# Merge datasets by participant identification number
full_df <- df_list %>%
  reduce(full_join, by = "SEQN")

dim(full_df)
```

The merged dataset contained 10,348 participants and 27 variables, 
including the participant identification number.

```{r, eval=FALSE}
# Optional: Export the merged, uncleaned dataset
write_csv(full_df, "full_health_data_2005_2006.csv")
```

## Initial Data Exploration

We examined the structure of the dataset and the proportion of 
missing observations for each variable to identify potential 
data-cleaning concerns.

```{r, results='hide'}
str(full_df)
```

```{r}
# Calculate the percentage of missing observations
round(colMeans(is.na(full_df)) * 100, 2)
```

Several variables had substantial missingness, particularly education 
level and medical conditions. This was likely due in part to differences 
in age eligibility across NHANES questionnaire modules.

## Restricting the Study Population

We restricted our analysis to adults aged 20 years and older because 
several demographic and medical condition variables were only 
collected for adults. This also allowed us to examine self-rated 
health within a more consistent study population.

```{r}
adult_df <- full_df %>%
  filter(RIDAGEYR >= 20)

dim(adult_df)

# Reexamine missingness after restricting the sample
round(colMeans(is.na(adult_df)) * 100, 2)
```

Restricting the dataset to adults reduced the sample size to 4,979 
participants. Missingness decreased substantially for education, 
physical activity, diabetes, and most medical conditions, indicating 
that much of the original missingness was related to age eligibility.

## Recoding Missing Responses

The `nhanesA` package provided categorical variables with descriptive 
factor levels. Responses of "Refused" and "Don't know" were recoded 
as missing values because they did not provide meaningful information 
about the corresponding variables.

```{r, results='hide'}
# Examine response categories and frequencies
lapply(adult_df, function(x) {
  if (is.factor(x)) table(x, useNA = "ifany")
})
```

```{r}
# Convert invalid categorical responses to NA
clean_df <- adult_df %>%
  mutate(across(where(is.factor), ~ {
    x <- as.character(.x)
    x[tolower(x) %in% c("refused", "don't know")] <- NA
    factor(x)
  }))
```

## Preparing the Response Variable

Self-rated general health (HUQ010) consists of five naturally ordered 
categories ranging from excellent to poor health. We converted the 
response variable to an ordered factor to preserve this ordinal 
structure for statistical modeling.

```{r}
clean_df$HUQ010 <- factor(
  clean_df$HUQ010,
  levels = c("Excellent,", "Very good,", "Good,", "Fair, or", "Poor?"),
  labels = c("Excellent", "Very good", "Good", "Fair", "Poor"),
  ordered = TRUE
)

table(clean_df$HUQ010, useNA = "always")
```

Five participants responded that they did not know their general 
health status. These participants were excluded because a valid 
response variable is necessary for model development and validation.

```{r}
clean_df <- clean_df %>%
  filter(!is.na(HUQ010))

dim(clean_df)
```

Following the exclusion of these participants, 4,974 observations 
remained in the dataset.

## Recoding Predictor Variables

### Asthma

The original asthma variable (MCQ035) had substantial missingness 
because the question was only administered to participants who had 
previously reported an asthma diagnosis. We replaced this variable 
with MCQ010, which indicates whether a participant has ever been 
diagnosed with asthma.

```{r recode-asthma}
# Select history of asthma diagnosis
asthma <- mcq_raw %>%
  select(SEQN, MCQ010)

# Replace the current asthma variable with asthma diagnosis history
clean_df <- clean_df %>%
  select(-MCQ035) %>%
  left_join(asthma, by = "SEQN")

# Set No as the reference and recode unknown responses as NA
clean_df$MCQ010 <- factor(
  clean_df$MCQ010,
  levels = c("No", "Yes")
)

table(clean_df$MCQ010, useNA = "always")
```

Replacing MCQ035 with MCQ010 substantially reduced asthma-related 
missingness, leaving only seven missing observations.

### Diabetes

Diabetes status was retained as a categorical variable with three 
levels: no diabetes, diabetes, and borderline diabetes. Borderline 
diabetes was retained as a separate category because it may have a 
different association with self-rated health.

```{r recode-diabetes}
clean_df$DIQ010 <- factor(
  clean_df$DIQ010,
  levels = c("No", "Yes", "Borderline")
)

table(clean_df$DIQ010, useNA = "always")
```

### Physical Activity

For moderate and muscle-strengthening physical activity, participants 
who reported being unable to perform the activity were retained as 
a separate category. Physical inability may have a different 
relationship with self-rated health than choosing not to participate.

```{r recode-activity}
clean_df <- clean_df %>%
  mutate(
    PAD320 = factor(
      PAD320,
      levels = c("No", "Yes", "Unable to do activity")
    ),
    PAD440 = factor(
      PAD440,
      levels = c("No", "Yes", "Unable to do activity")
    )
  )
```

The response categories for usual daily physical activity were 
shortened to improve the readability of tables and model results.

```{r relabel-daily-activity}
clean_df$PAQ180 <- factor(
  clean_df$PAQ180,
  levels = levels(adult_df$PAQ180)[1:4],
  labels = c(
    "Mostly sitting",
    "Standing or walking",
    "Light lifting or climbing",
    "Heavy work"
  )
)
```

### Medical Conditions

For each medical condition, "No" was established as the reference 
category to allow comparisons between participants with and 
without the condition.

```{r medical-reference-levels}
clean_df <- clean_df %>%
  mutate(across(starts_with("MCQ"),
                ~ factor(.x, levels = c("No", "Yes"))))
```

### Demographic Characteristics

Reference categories were established for the categorical demographic 
predictors to facilitate interpretation of the regression coefficients.

```{r demographic-reference-levels}
clean_df <- clean_df %>%
  mutate(
    RIAGENDR = relevel(RIAGENDR, ref = "Male"),
    RIDRETH1 = relevel(RIDRETH1, ref = "Non-Hispanic White"),
    DMDEDUC2 = relevel(DMDEDUC2, ref = "College Graduate or above"),
    DMDMARTL = relevel(DMDMARTL, ref = "Married")
  )
```

## Remaining Missing Data

After recoding the selected variables, we examined the remaining 
missing observations and determined how many participants had 
complete responses across all candidate variables.

```{r remaining-missingness}
round(colMeans(is.na(clean_df)) * 100, 2)

sum(complete.cases(clean_df))
```

Following data cleaning, 4,159 participants had complete responses 
across all currently selected variables. Waist circumference and 
the family income-to-poverty ratio accounted for substantial 
portions of the remaining missingness.

## Preparing the Analytic Dataset

We retained the family income-to-poverty ratio (INDFMPIR) rather 
than total household income (INDHHINC) because it accounts for 
family size when measuring economic circumstances. Retaining both 
variables could also introduce redundancy into the model.

```{r}
analysis_df <- clean_df %>%
  select(-INDHHINC)

sum(complete.cases(analysis_df))
```

After removing household income, 4,170 participants had complete 
responses across all remaining candidate predictors.

## Training and Testing Datasets

To evaluate the final model's predictive performance on observations 
not used during model development, we randomly divided the dataset 
into an 80% training dataset and a 20% testing dataset. We established 
a random seed to ensure reproducibility.

```{r}
set.seed(443)

train_index <- sample(
  seq_len(nrow(analysis_df)),
  size = floor(0.80 * nrow(analysis_df))
)

train_df <- analysis_df[train_index, ]
test_df <- analysis_df[-train_index, ]

dim(train_df)
dim(test_df)
```

The training dataset contained 3,979 participants, while the testing 
dataset contained 995 participants. Incomplete predictor observations 
were retained at this stage because the final predictors had not yet 
been selected. The testing dataset was held out from exploratory 
analysis, model development, and model selection.

## Exploratory Data Analysis

Exploratory data analysis was conducted using only the training 
dataset to examine relationships between potential predictors 
and self-rated general health. Cross-tabulations and conditional 
percentages were used to compare the distribution of self-rated 
health across predictor categories.

### Diabetes and Self-Rated Health

We first examined the relationship between diabetes status and 
self-rated general health.

```{r diabetes-health-table}
# Cross-tabulate diabetes status and self-rated health
table(train_df$DIQ010, train_df$HUQ010)

# Calculate row percentages
round(prop.table(
  table(train_df$DIQ010, train_df$HUQ010), 1
) * 100, 2)
```

Participants with diabetes generally reported poorer health than participants without diabetes. Approximately 49.61% of participants with diabetes reported fair or poor health, compared with 18.15% of participants without diabetes. Participants with borderline diabetes also reported fair or poor health more frequently (37.31%) than participants without diabetes. These results suggest that diabetes status may be a valuable predictor of self-rated general health.

### Arthritis and Self-Rated Health

We examined whether the distribution of self-rated health differed 
between participants with and without a history of arthritis.

```{r arthritis-health-table}
# Cross-tabulate arthritis and self-rated health
table(train_df$MCQ160A, train_df$HUQ010)

# Calculate row percentages
round(prop.table(
  table(train_df$MCQ160A, train_df$HUQ010), 1
) * 100, 2)
```

Participants with arthritis generally reported poorer health than participants without arthritis. Approximately 35.83% of participants with arthritis reported fair or poor health, compared with 16.88% of participants without arthritis. This suggests that arthritis may be an important predictor of self-rated general health.

### Physical Activity and Self-Rated Health

We examined the relationship between usual daily physical activity 
and self-rated general health to determine whether health ratings 
differed across activity levels.

```{r activity-health-table}
# Cross-tabulate daily activity level and self-rated health
table(train_df$PAQ180, train_df$HUQ010)

# Calculate row percentages
round(prop.table(
  table(train_df$PAQ180, train_df$HUQ010), 1
) * 100, 2)
```

Participants who reported sitting for most of the day generally reported poorer health than participants with more active daily routines. Approximately 32.02% of participants who primarily sat throughout the day reported fair or poor health, compared with 12.94% of participants who frequently performed light lifting or climbing. These findings suggest that usual daily physical activity level may be associated with self-rated general health.

### Relationships Between Predictors

We also examined relationships among the selected categorical 
predictors to identify potential associations and redundancy.

```{r predictor-relationships}
# Diabetes status and arthritis
round(prop.table(
  table(train_df$DIQ010, train_df$MCQ160A), 1
) * 100, 2)

# Diabetes status and daily physical activity
round(prop.table(
  table(train_df$DIQ010, train_df$PAQ180), 1
) * 100, 2)

# Arthritis and daily physical activity
round(prop.table(
  table(train_df$MCQ160A, train_df$PAQ180), 1
) * 100, 2)
```

Associations were also observed among diabetes status, arthritis, and daily physical activity. Approximately 48.35% of participants with diabetes reported having arthritis, compared with 21.86% of participants without diabetes. Additionally, participants with diabetes or arthritis were more likely to report sitting for most of the day than participants without these conditions. Although these relationships suggest some overlap among the predictors, each variable measures a distinct aspect of health. Therefore, all three predictors were retained for further evaluation during model selection.
